state: shared memory[information generated by the different agents] of the workflow, 
class ResearchState(TypedDict):
    user_query: str
    research_plan: dict
    research_output: dict
    analysis_output: dict
    validation_output: dict
    final_report: dict

Each agent reads the information it needs and returns updates to the shared State.

Node:
A Node is a function that performs a particular task in the workflow.
Your five agents will become five workflow nodes.
Node	Function
planner	Calls Planner Agent
researcher	Calls Research Agent
analyst	Calls Analysis Agent
validator	Calls Validation Agent
report_writer	Calls Report Writer Agent

Remember: Agent = performs the intelligence/task. Node = connects that task to the workflow.


Edges – Connections between nodes
An Edge determines which node executes next.

ex:workflow.add_edge("planner", "researcher")
workflow.add_edge("researcher", "analyst")
workflow.add_edge("analyst", "validator")

his creates the execution sequence:

Planner → Researcher → Analyst → Validator.


Conditional Edges – Decision making
This is where LangGraph becomes especially useful.

Suppose your Validation Agent returns:
overall_status = "needs_revision"

You want the workflow to return to the Analyst.

But if it returns "pass", you want to generate the report.


Concept	Meaning
START	Beginning of workflow
END	Completion of workflow
compile()	Builds the graph into an executable workflow
invoke()	Starts executing the workflow


